potenuse is $2\\sqrt{6}$ centimeters?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"25caf0f5e15aa6135b9369ac574cd952f12d6837acbbe08eb10e7a8f6033e799"},"seconds":8.879880428314209,"seed":1023420578466146683,"stop":"eos","text":"To solve the problem, we need to use the properties of a 30-60-90 right triangle. In such a triangle, the sides are in the ratio \\(1 : \\sqrt{3} : 2\\), where the hypotenuse is the longest side and is twice the length of the shortest side (the side opposite the 30-degree angle), and the side opposite the 60-degree angle is \\(\\sqrt{3}\\) times the length of the shortest side.\n\nGiven that the hypotenuse is \\(2\\sqrt{6}\\) cm, we can denote the shortest side (opposite the 30-degree angle) as \\(x\\). According to the properties of a 30-60-90 triangle, the hypotenuse is \\(2x\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[2x = 2\\sqrt{6}\\]\nSolving for \\(x\\), we get:\n\\[x = \\sqrt{6}\\]\n\nThe side opposite the 60-degree angle is \\(x\\sqrt{3}\\). Substituting \\(x = \\sqrt{6}\\), we get:\n\\[x\\sqrt{3} = \\sqrt{6} \\cdot \\sqrt{3} = \\sqrt{18} = 3\\sqrt{2}\\]\n\nThe two legs of the triangle are \\(x = \\sqrt{6}\\) and \\(x\\sqrt{3} = 3\\sqrt{2}\\). The sum of the lengths of the two legs is:\n\\[\\sqrt{6} + 3\\sqrt{2}\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + 3\\sqrt{2}}\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{\\sqrt{6} + 3\\sqrt{2}}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"prealgebra:626"}
